SteamARM: a game's FSR 1 upscale done by MetalFX

FSR 1 enlarges a game's 3D picture in two passes, EASU (the upscale) and
RCAS (a sharpening). A fragment shader with the two constants of EASU's
approximations (0x7ef07ebb, 0x5f347d74) is marked when it is compiled
(kk_shader_info.fsr_easu, kept by the pipeline caches); its draw is then
replaced by MetalFX's spatial scaler (MTL4FXSpatialScaler) from the
shader's input texture into the pass's colour attachment, in the same
Metal command buffer: the pass's encoder ends first (its clears and stores
happen), MetalFX follows, and the next encoder waits for all the work
before it (a queue barrier, after_metalfx). RCAS still runs on MetalFX's
result; the HUD drawn afterwards stays at the display's resolution.

The input is found from the descriptor (set 1 binding 30 in Counter-Strike
2's EASU, the sampler at binding 14, the constants at binding 1): image
views register the resource id of their sampled texture. Formats or texture
usage MetalFX cannot take, or no Metal 4 MetalFX: the game's own pass.
KK_FSR_METALFX=0: the game's own pass always. tests/elf/vk_fsr.c checks the
replacement with a shader of its own (magenta when drawn, the input
enlarged when MetalFX takes it).
